    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190913": {
      "title": "Assistant Research Professor, Department of Performing Arts and Health",
      "description": "Apply - Interfolio undefined page loaded Skip to Main Content Already have an account? Sign In Assistant Research Professor, Department of Performing Arts and Health Johns Hopkins University: Peabody Institute Location Baltimore, MD, 21202 Open Date Aug 07, 2026 Salary Range or Pay Grade $75,000 - $90,000 Description The Department of Performing Arts and Health at the Peabody Institute of the Johns Hopkins University invites applications for the position of Assistant Research Professor . This is a full-time one-year position that can be renewed up to 2 times. The successful candidate will bring a track record of research, academic, and professional activity at the intersection of music/dance and health and will be prepared to contribute to the department’s interdisciplinary research, teach undergraduate and/or graduate-level courses, mentor students, assist with new program development, and engage in departmental service. While the appointment is at the Peabody Institute, there is the potential for research affiliation and collaboration with other divisions of Johns Hopkins University. The Assistant Research Professor will collaborate with departmental faculty and staff at Conservatory, teaching artist faculty at Preparatory, and clinicians in the Johns Hopkins Rehabilitation Network’s Clinic for Performing Artists at the Peabody Institute. This is an excellent opportunity for an early-career researcher seeking to establish a productive research agenda and contribute to the academic development of students, the department, and an emerging Center for Performing Arts and Health. Candidates with interdisciplinary experience and a background in music or dance are particularly encouraged to apply. Areas of research priority include: · Occupational health in music and dance: injury prevention​, health promotion, voice science, dance science, occupational health · Optimizing artistic performance: performance science, performance optimization, sports medicine, performance psychology, kinesiology, physiology, voice science, dance science, music and dance pedagogy · Optimizing recovery and quality of life through music and dance: Adaptive arts, creative arts therapies, arts in health, music-assisted therapy, physical medicine and rehabilitation, rehabilitation psychology · Precision healthcare for performing artists: ​ performing arts medicine, clinical research, epidemiology, clinical competencies, physician and clinician training Responsibilities Research Conduct independent and collaborative research to advance the department’s interdisciplinary research agenda. Publish findings in peer-reviewed journals and present research at academic conferences. Contribute to research grant proposals to secure external funding to support research projects. Contribute to the development of research culture, infrastructure, and capacity building within a conservatory context. Lead and collaborate with interdisciplinary research teams to address emerging questions at the intersection of the arts and health in educational, clinical, and arts practices. Supervise and direct research activities of undergraduate students, graduate students, and postdoctoral fellows. Teaching Teach undergraduate- and graduate-level courses in Performing Arts and Health, including Intro to Research in Performing Arts and Health, Foundations of Health Promotion for Performing Artists, and/or others according to demand and areas of expertise. Contribute to the development of course materials, syllabi, and assessments. Foster an inclusive and engaging classroom environment that encourages the development of research capacity. Supervise graduate student thesis/dissertation projects as appropriate. Provide regular feedback and guidance to students, ensuring a positive and health-promoting learning experience. Mentorship Provide mentorship to graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, and clinicians, fostering their academic and professional development. Guide students in their academic and career development at the intersection of music/dance and health. Actively participate in departmental seminars, workshops, and other scholarly events to support students’ learning and professional growth. Offer support for students applying for research funding, fellowships, and postdoctoral positions. Administrative Contribute to the academic and administrative activities of the department, including attending departmental meetings and committees. Assist in organizing departmental research events including seminars, workshops, and conferences. Participate in designing, developing, implementing, and evaluating new Performance Clinic and/or Arts in Community Health program pilots, according to areas of expertise. Support the department's outreach and communication efforts, including collaborations with external research institutions and industry partners. Provide service to the university and professional community, including reviewing papers for journals, serving on grant panels, and participating in professional societies. Assist with student recruitment, including promoting the department to prospective students. Qualifications Required qualifications: A doctoral degree in Music/Dance Therapy, Education, Physical Therapy, Occupational Therapy, Kinesiology, Physiology, Psychology, Medicine, Nursing, Public Health, or related discipline or equivalent, completed by the time of appointment. A strong research record with publications in peer-reviewed journals in one or more of the following areas: performance science; musicians’/dancers’ occupational health; clinical treatment of performing artists; music/dance therapy; adaptive arts; the application of music and dance in healthcare, public health, or community health; and/or arts prescribing. Experience in teaching and mentoring graduate students. Proven ability to secure research funding through grants or other external sources. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Evidence of ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a team environment. Preferred Qualifications Experience with the specific research methodologies, tools, or technologies associated with the candidate’s field. A strong publication record in top-tier journals. Demonstrated ability to lead and manage research projects. Prior teaching experience at the graduate level. Experience with interdisciplinary collaboration and research. Arts administration or arts entrepreneurship experience. Background in music or dance. Application Instructions Interested candidates should submit the following materials: A cover letter detailing research interests, teaching philosophy, and professional goals. Curriculum Vitae (CV), including a list of publications and research funding history. A research statement outlining current and future research plans. A teaching statement, including a summary of teaching experience and philosophy. Contact information for at least three professional references. For questions, please contact Sarah Hoover, Associate Dean for Innovation in the Arts and Health and Chair, Department of Performing Arts and Health, sarah.hoover@jhu.edu . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct\n            this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier\n            account and can send all application materials, including\n            confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Salary Range The referenced salary range represents the minimum and maximum salaries for this position and is based on Johns Hopkins University's good faith belief at the time of posting. Not all candidates will be eligible for the upper end of the salary range. The actual compensation offered to the selected candidate may vary and will ultimately depend on multiple factors, which may include the successful candidate's geographic location, skills, work experience, internal equity, market conditions, education/training and other factors, as reasonably determined by the University. Total Rewards Johns Hopkins offers a total rewards package that supports our employees' health, life, career and retirement. More information can be found here: https://hr.jhu.edu/benefits-worklife/ . Equal Opportunity Employer The Johns Hopkins University is committed to equal opportunity for its faculty, staff, and students. To that end, the university does not discriminate on the basis of sex, gender, marital status, pregnancy, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, age, disability, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, veteran status or other legally protected characteristics. The University is committed to providing qualified individuals access to all academic and employment programs, benefits and activities on the basis of demonstrated ability, performance and merit without regard to personal factors or demographic characteristics that are irrelevant to the program involved. Pre-Employment Information If you are interested in applying for employment with Johns Hopkins University and require special assistance or accommodation during any part of the pre-employment process, please contact the HR Business Services Office at jhurecruitment@jhu.edu . For TTY users, call via Maryland Relay or dial 711. For more information about workplace accommodations at Johns Hopkins University for disabilities, medical conditions (including medical conditions related to pregnancy or childbirth), accessibility, or religious reasons, please visit accessibility.jhu.edu . Background Checks After receiving a conditional offer, the successful candidate(s) for this position will be subject to a pre-employment background check including education verification.  When deciding whether a candidate’s conviction history is job-disqualifying, the University considers the nature and gravity of the offense, the time that has passed since the conviction, and the nature of the job being sought. EEO is the Law https://www.eeoc.gov/employees-job-applicants Vaccine Requirements Johns Hopkins University strongly encourages, but no longer requires, at least one dose of the COVID-19 vaccine. This change does not apply to the School of Medicine (SOM). SOM hires must be fully vaccinated with an FDA COVID-19 vaccination and provide proof of vaccination status. We still require all faculty, staff, and students to receive the seasonal flu vaccine . Exceptions to the seasonal flu vaccine or COVID-19 vaccine (for SOM) requirement(s) may be provided to individuals with sincerely held religious beliefs or medical conditions that preclude them from receiving the vaccine. Requests for an exception must be submitted to the JHU vaccination registry. For additional information, applicants for SOM positions should visit https://www.hopkinsmedicine.org/coronavirus/covid-19-vaccine/ and all other JHU applicants should visit https://covidinfo.jhu.edu/health-safety/covid-vaccination-information/ . The following additional vaccine requirements may apply, depending upon your campus. Please contact the hiring department for more information. The pre-employment physical for positions in clinical areas, laboratories, working with research subjects, or involving community contact requires documentation of immune status against Rubella (German measles), Rubeola (Measles), Mumps, Varicella (chickenpox), Hepatitis B and documentation of having received the Tdap (Tetanus, diphtheria, pertussis) vaccination. This may include documentation of having two (2) MMR vaccines; two (2) Varicella vaccines; or antibody status to these diseases from laboratory testing. Blood tests for immunities to these diseases are ordinarily included in the pre-employment physical exam except for those candidates who provide results of blood tests or immunization documentation from their own health care providers.  Any vaccinations required for these diseases will be given at no cost in our Occupational Health office. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. or its licensors and contributors. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies. Support | Careers | Accessibility Policy | Cookies Settings | Privacy Policy | Terms of Service We use cookies that are necessary to make our site work. We may also use additional cookies to analyze, improve, and personalize our content and your digital experience. You can manage your cookie preferences using the Cookie settings link. For more information, see our Cookie Policy Cookie settings Accept all cookies Opt-Out Request Honored Cookie Preference Center We use cookies which are necessary to make our site work. We may also use additional cookies to analyse, improve and personalise our content and your digital experience. For more information, see our Cookie Policy and the list of Google Ad-Tech Vendors . You may choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types may impact your experience of our site and the services we are able to offer. See the different category headings below to find out more or change your settings. You may also be able to exercise your privacy choices as described in our Privacy Policy Allow all Manage Consent Preferences Strictly Necessary Cookies Always active These cookies are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems. They are usually only set in response to actions made by you which amount to a request for services, such as setting your privacy preferences, logging in or filling in forms. You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work. Cookie Details List‎ Functional Cookies Functional Cookies These cookies enable the website to provide enhanced functionality and personalisation. They may be set by us or by third party providers whose services we have added to our pages. If you do not allow these cookies then some or all of these services may not function properly. Cookie Details List‎ Performance Cookies Performance Cookies These cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources so we can measure and improve the performance of our site. They help us to know which pages are the most and least popular and see how visitors move around the site. Cookie Details List‎ Targeting Cookies Targeting Cookies These cookies may be set through our site by our advertising partners. They may be used by those companies to build a profile of your interests and show you relevant adverts on other sites. If you do not allow these cookies, you will experience less targeted advertising. Cookie Details List‎ Back Button Cookie List Search Icon Filter Icon Clear checkbox label label Apply Cancel Consent Leg.Interest checkbox label label checkbox label label checkbox label label Confirm my choices"
