    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188898": {
      "title": "Apply",
      "description": "Apply - Interfolio undefined page loaded Skip to Main Content Already have an account? Sign In Adjunct Faculty, MS in Financial Economics Johns Hopkins University: Zanvyl Krieger School of Arts and Sciences: Advanced Academic Programs Location Washington, DC, 20001 Open Date Jun 30, 2026 Salary Range or Pay Grade $8,755 - $9,270 Description Institution Johns Hopkins University Krieger School of Arts and Sciences Advanced Academic Programs About Advanced Academic Programs Advanced Academic Programs (AAP) is a Johns Hopkins University’s Krieger School of Arts and Sciences division. As part of the nation’s first research university, AAP offers rigorous and high-quality master’s degrees at the Hopkins Bloomberg Center in Washington, DC, at the Homewood campus in Baltimore, Maryland, and online. AAP distinguishes itself through its intensive instructional assistance, selective admissions, over 60 dedicated full-time faculty. Tenured Krieger School faculty members serve as program chairs to oversee the academic rigor of AAP’s graduate offerings. For more information, please see http://advanced.jhu.edu/ . Position Description The MS in Financial Economics program at Johns Hopkins University is seeking applications to establish a pool of qualified adjunct instructors for future teaching opportunities. While appointments are based on program needs, we anticipate hiring additional adjunct faculty in upcoming semesters. We are particularly interested in instructors qualified to teach one or more of the following courses: Financial Economics Financial Econometrics Financial Intermediation and Financial Markets Economics of Investments and Financial Management Behavioral Economics and Finance Economics of Derivatives Other graduate-level finance and economics courses aligned with the program curriculum The ideal candidate has a passion for graduate education and its power to transform the careers of working professionals, international students, and nontraditional learners. Successful candidates will have a demonstrated record of effective graduate (in-person and/or online) teaching and a commitment to creating an engaging and inclusive learning environment. As the finance profession continues to evolve with advances in artificial intelligence and data analytics, we are especially interested in instructors who can thoughtfully incorporate AI tools and AI-related skills into their teaching where appropriate. Examples include using AI to support financial analysis, data visualization, programming, forecasting, risk analysis, investment research, and decision-making, while also helping students understand the opportunities and limitations of these technologies. The expected salary range for this position is: $8,755 - $9,270 per course. The referenced salary range is based on Johns Hopkins University's good faith belief at the time of posting. The actual compensation offered to the selected candidate may vary and will be based on factors including, but not limited to, the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate – e.g., years in rank, training, field, discipline, other work experience, and other similar factors; geographic location; internal equity; external market conditions; and other factors as reasonably determined by the University. Qualifications Minimum Qualifications Master's degree in Economics, Finance, or a closely related field. At least one year of graduate-level teaching experience (in-person, online, or hybrid). At least one year of relevant professional experience in economics, finance, banking, investments, risk management, or a related field. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. in Economics, Finance, or a closely related field. Several years of graduate-level teaching experience. Significant industry experience in finance or economics. Experience integrating AI tools, data analytics, programming, or other emerging technologies into finance or economics instruction. Experience designing applied learning activities that connect theory with current industry practice. Application Instructions The position will remain open until it is filled. Candidates must submit the following: Cover letter CV Teaching evaluations for the three most recently taught course Teaching statement (1–2 pages) describing how to incorporate AI into a graduate finance course. Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct\n            this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier\n            account and can send all application materials, including\n            confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Salary Range The referenced salary range represents the minimum and maximum salaries for this position and is based on Johns Hopkins University's good faith belief at the time of posting. Not all candidates will be eligible for the upper end of the salary range. The actual compensation offered to the selected candidate may vary and will ultimately depend on multiple factors, which may include the successful candidate's geographic location, skills, work experience, internal equity, market conditions, education/training and other factors, as reasonably determined by the University. Total Rewards Johns Hopkins offers a total rewards package that supports our employees' health, life, career and retirement. More information can be found here: https://hr.jhu.edu/benefits-worklife/ . Equal Opportunity Employer The Johns Hopkins University is committed to equal opportunity for its faculty, staff, and students. To that end, the university does not discriminate on the basis of sex, gender, marital status, pregnancy, race, color, ethnicity, national origin, age, disability, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, veteran status or other legally protected characteristics. The University is committed to providing qualified individuals access to all academic and