    "https://apply.interfolio.com/176173": {
      "title": "Apply",
      "description": "Apply - Interfolio undefined page loaded Skip to Main Content Already have an account? Sign In Attending Physician- Department of Emergency Medicine Johns Hopkins University: School of Medicine: Department of Emergency Medicine Location Columbia, MD, 21044 Open Date Oct 20, 2025 Salary Range or Pay Grade $300,000-$400,000 annually Description The Johns Hopkins University Department of Emergency Medicine is seeking an Attending Physician for their location at Johns Hopkins Howard County Medical Center. The role of the physician will be as a Clinical Associate within the busy and diverse department, serving Columbia, Maryland and the surrounding county. The Emergency Department sees upwards of 60,000 patients annually. The department is equipped with a triage area, green zone fast track space, seperate behavioral health unit and a 36 bed main ED Space. We are looking for a physician who is ready for a diverse subset of diagnoses, high acuity, and fast paced ED setting. Qualifications Physician must have completed their residency in Emergency Medicine and be board certified in Emergency Medicine. For those candidates just out of residency, boards must be passed within one year of employment. Must maintain a Maryland license, DEA, and CDS. Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct\n            this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier\n            account and can send all application materials, including\n            confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Salary Range The referenced salary range represents the minimum and maximum salaries for this position and is based on Johns Hopkins University's good faith belief at the time of posting. Not all candidates will be eligible for the upper end of the salary range. The actual compensation offered to the selected candidate may vary and will ultimately depend on multiple fa